guitar hero live is a complex machine  with a lot of moving parts and a lot of  new ideas something both the franchise  and the entire rhythm game genre  desperately needs some of these new  ideas really work while others failed  pretty miserably  which makes Guitar Hero's long-awaited  return a consuming often overwhelming  sequence of incredible highs and  unbearable loads the game offers two  completely separate experiences in the  form of a traditional structured  campaign and an ever evolving music  platform that basically layers gameplay  on top of official music videos that  first component like the game itself is  called Guitar Hero live presumably  because it sets each of its 42 tracks  against first-person style footage of  real-life actors in theory this should  instantly immerse players in the  Rockstar fantasy of playing on stage and  for thousands of fans but one I was  generally too focused on the gameplay to  notice everything happening in the  background and to when I did notice I  frequently just cringed everything from  the costumes to the acting to the  unskipable concert intros feels  painfully forced and inauthentic every  single person smiles aggressively with  canned enthusiasm while giving you the  most comically exaggerated thumbs-up  you've ever seen it's so campy it  actually borders on parody - the cheeky  self-awareness and weirdly crowd  reactions come in just two flavors good  and bad depending on your performance  you're either an irredeemable  embarrassment or an untouchable rock god  there is no in-between  I appreciate lives attempt to do  something truly different and the new  approach did provide a few memorable  moments late in the campaign but the  execution basically ruins would go to  potentially been a cool idea thankfully  lives other major change pretty much  single-handedly redeems the entire game  it's new side-by-side button  configuration fundamentally changes the  way the game plays and delivers some  desperately needed innovation to a  stagnant Jarrah even seasoned music game  vets have to start from scratch which  renews the sense of reward that comes so  naturally as your skills develop that  sense of challenge and progress is a  huge part of the genres core appeal so  to completely revitalize those claws  is an unbelievable accomplishment that  said live doesn't completely nail the  new gameplay Ratatat the gate similar  sounds and guitar techniques manifest  differently in different songs which  made the learning process a little more  frustrating I also occasionally felt  like what I was playing didn't totally  match up with what I was hearing  the biggest issue though is the music  tastes are subjective sure but even if  you love every artist on Live's roster  many of the songs just aren't cut out  for Guitar Hero gameplay way too often I  found myself playing one simple  repeating hook over and over because  honestly that's how a lot of pop songs  are structured unlike rock songs they  were never intended to showcase the  guitarist artists like Skrillex and  Eminem are undoubtedly popular but their  tracks just don't make sense for a  guitar driven experience this brings us  to guitar hero lives other half Guitar  Hero TV which offers a far more  substantial library while the campaign's  42 on disc tracks consist almost  uniformly of top 40 artists from the  past five or so years gh TV includes old  six-string standards like Weezer the  clash and Pantera but there is a catch  any time you select a song from the now  playing section you have to spend a play  a 10 pack of plays will only cost you  about 2.50 cents and you can buy  unlimited plays for 24 hours for 6  dollars but you can easily avoid  spending money altogether by earning  in-game currency while playing gh TV's  channels which like the ancient days of  MTV play a constant pre-programmed  stream of music that you can jump in and  jam along with yeah you'll also earn  chunks of free plays periodically just  for playing the game at all although  there is no way to permanently unlock a  song so yes you can technically play gh  TV without spending money and you will  have access to its 200 plus songs if you  want to spend money the rates aren't  terrible and they might even make sense  for players who used to buy a DLC track  and then only play it once or twice but  honestly it's uncomfortable to even have  to consider whether a song is really  worth a play or to be forced to endure  content I don't care about simply to  access the content I want it changed the  way I thought about the entire  experience and for me  that lack of freedom undermined a lot of  the fun in spite of the campaign's  off-putting presentation and GH TVs  unpleasant microtransactions guitar hero  lives updated mechanics make music  driven gameplay fresh and fun again it  doesn't erase the game's problems but it  is a compelling reason to grab a new axe  and rock out regardless  you
 

